# Client env. Only VITE_-prefixed keys reach the browser bundle, and Vite
# INLINES them at build time — so every value here is public, and changing one
# requires a rebuild + redeploy (deploy.sh runs `npm run build` on the server).
#
# NOTE: this file is committed to git. Never put a secret in it.
# `npm run build` fails if a non-VITE_ key appears here
# (scripts/check-env-secrets.mjs).

# --- Lead capture (EmailJS) -------------------------------------------------
# Every form sends through the Gmail account you connect in the EmailJS
# dashboard. There is no SMTP password anywhere: EmailJS holds the OAuth
# connection, so nothing secret exists on our side to leak.
#
# All three values below are PUBLIC by design — the public key can only trigger
# the template you defined, with the content you defined. What actually stops
# another site spending your quota is the origin allowlist:
#   Dashboard → Account → Security → Domains → add innoviaburst.com
#
# WHY THE SMTP SETTINGS ARE NOT IN THIS FILE
# @emailjs/browser's API is send(serviceId, templateId, params, {publicKey}).
# It takes no host/port/user/pass — the SMTP connection lives on EmailJS's
# servers, configured once in their dashboard. A browser cannot open a TCP
# socket, so no library can speak SMTP from a page regardless; and a password
# in a VITE_ variable is inlined into the public bundle for anyone to read.
#
# Your MAIL_* values still have a home — in the dashboard, not here:
#   MAIL_HOST          → Service → SMTP Server → Host
#   MAIL_PORT          → Service → SMTP Server → Port
#   MAIL_SECURE        → Service → SMTP Server → Use SSL/TLS
#   MAIL_USER          → Service → SMTP Server → Username
#   MAIL_PASS          → Service → SMTP Server → Password
#   MAIL_FROM_NAME     → Template → From Name
#   MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS  → Template → From Email
#   MAIL_REPLY_TO      → Template → Reply To  (prefer {{reply_to}}, which is
#                        the lead's address, so Reply answers them not you)
#
# SETUP
#  1. Add the email service: Dashboard → Email Services → Add New Service.
#     Either 'Gmail' (OAuth — nothing secret to store, recommended) or
#     'SMTP Server' if you prefer to enter the MAIL_* values above.
#  2. Create ONE template (Dashboard → Email Templates). It must use these
#     variables, which src/lib/submit-lead.ts sends:
#
#       Subject:   {{subject}}
#       Reply To:  {{reply_to}}      <- makes Reply answer the lead, not you
#       Content:   {{message}}       <- the whole pre-formatted body
#
#     {{from_name}} and {{lead_type}} are also available if you want them.
#     {{message}} is REQUIRED — leave it out and the email arrives empty.
#  3. Paste the three ids below.
#
# Free tier is 200 sends/month.

# Dashboard → Email Services → your Gmail service
VITE_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=service_tjkblui
# Dashboard → Email Templates → the template from step 2
VITE_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=template_5de0d19
# Dashboard → Account → General → Public Key
VITE_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=N7PiAcfOIUVLE6_E3

# --- Scheduling -------------------------------------------------------------
# Link offered on the success screen after an enquiry is sent.
# Paste the BARE link, e.g. https://calendly.com/innoviaburst/15min
# Empty simply hides the button; the form still works and you reply by email.
# NOT embedded — see the comment in src/components/BookingModal.tsx.
VITE_SCHEDULING_URL=
